  "body": "Noted by the high-tier review of PR #1082 (review comment on that PR, head 642cd694); true on main before that PR as well, so not a regression of it.\n\n**What.** The index-swing check (#804 / #810 / #818, `crates/app/src/posting_index_swing.rs`) judges the tool against the work as the rotary indexes. It does not judge the swept WORK (part, stock, and what turns with them) against what stands still under it: the machine table, a fixture plate, a rotary subplate. A long or wide workpiece on a low rotary can swing into the table or plate during an index and the program posts.\n\nRelated gap from the same review: a rotary standing directly on a fixture plate with no subplate is judged on the bare-table figure (\"a plate alone raises nothing\", by design) with no named note.\n\n**Why P1, not P0, for now.** It is a collision the check does not look for, not one it looks for and gets wrong, and it is unknown how much of the table/plate geometry the check can read today (the machine file states `nose_to_axis`; the table's extent may not be stated at all). If the first step shows the figures are there and a real setup passes, re-rate it P0.\n\nDone means: it is stated (decision file) what the swing check judges and against which bodies; the swept work's largest radius about the rotary line is judged against the stated distance from the centerline to the table / plate / subplate top, with a refusal by name when it does not clear and a named \"not judged\" note when the machine states no figure to judge it against; pinned by tests with a workpiece that clears and one that does not.\n",
